.TH TIMEDELTA 1 "2026-09-25" "timedelta 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timedelta \- compute the difference between two points in time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timedelta
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-start\fR \fITEXT\fR]
[\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-end\fR \fITEXT\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timedelta
is a minimal command-line tool for computing the elapsed time between two
points in time. If
.B \-\-start
or
.B \-\-end
are not given, you will be prompted for them interactively.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-start\fR \fITEXT\fR
Start point in time. Accepts a full ISO 8601 datetime (e.g.
.IR 2024\-01\-01T10:00:00 ),
just a time of day for today (e.g.
.IR 10:00:00 ),
or the literal
.I now
for the current time. Leaving this blank when prompted also defaults to
the current time.
.IP
A timezone may be attached using a trailing
.I Z
for UTC, a numeric offset (e.g.
.IR +02:00 ),
or a space-separated IANA timezone name (e.g.
.IR "10:00:00 Europe/Oslo" ).
Only one of these may be used at a time; if omitted, naive/local time is
assumed.
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-end\fR \fITEXT\fR
End point in time. Accepts the same formats as
.BR \-\-start .
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fI{seconds,minutes,hours,fractions}\fR
Controls the level of detail in the output:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B seconds
\-\- total seconds, e.g. "9045 seconds"
.IP \(bu 2
.B minutes
\-\- minutes and seconds, e.g. "150 minutes, 45 seconds"
.IP \(bu 2
.B hours
\-\- hours, minutes and seconds (default), e.g. "2 hours, 30 minutes, 45 seconds"
.IP \(bu 2
.B fractions
\-\- a fractional number of hours, e.g. "2.5 hours"
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Show the installed version and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR
Show a summary of options and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Compute the delta between two full datetimes:
.B timedelta \-\-start 2024\-01\-01T10:00:00 \-\-end 2024\-01\-01T12:30:00
.TP
Compute the time since a given time today:
.B timedelta \-s 08:49 \-e now
.TP
Compute the time remaining until a given time today:
.B timedelta \-s now \-e 20:00:00
.TP
Report the delta as a fractional number of hours:
.B timedelta \-s 10:00:00 \-e 12:30:00 \-f fractions
.SH AUTHOR
Stig B. Dørmænen <stigbd@gmail.com>
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation and source: <https://github.com/stigbd/timedelta>
